{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305120,"nonce":"6W//wbCCD+RdivLK3dTI94SMfkU=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPhcdQ","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/3","origin":"github"},"title":"Pieces spanning file boundaries can never verify","message":"`check_file_chunk()` opens a single candidate file, seeks, and reads `piece_length` bytes:\n\n```python\nwith open(filename, \"rb\") as sfile:\n    sfile.seek(offset)\n    piece = sfile.read(length)\n    return hashlib.sha1(piece).digest() == hash\n```\n\nIn a multi-file torrent the piece stream is the concatenation of all files. A piece starting near the end of file A continues into file B. Reading `piece_length` from A alone short-reads at EOF, so the SHA-1 is computed over a truncated buffer and **can never match**.\n\nConsequences:\n- Every piece straddling a file boundary is reported bad, for every candidate.\n- Those bytes are only ever recovered by the whole-file copy of the \"biggest\" candidate, unverified.\n- Torrents with many small files are affected worst.\n\nThe README currently describes this as an inherent property of the format (\"sometimes it's impossible to check md5 sum of first and last chunk\"). It is not — it is this bug. Real torrents make it easy to reproduce: in `BigBuckBunny_124_archive.torrent`, 9 of 12 files are smaller than the 512 KiB piece length, so the opening pieces span up to seven files at once.\n\nFixing this requires modelling the torrent as one global byte stream and reading a piece from all files it covers.","files":null}]}